[{:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-49661"] :question/problem "Does the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡n}{{n}^{2}}$ for $n\\ge 1$ converge or diverge? If the sequence converges, what is its limit?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nWe calculate the limit of the sequence as $n$ approaches infinity:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡n}{{n}^{2}}\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡n}{{n}^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sequence converges,  and its limit is $0.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-49734/selection/8e921bffb50a4e46de83ddac3f98a24bb71d1e68ac0b40cb4fc363fa694725df" :answer/id #uuid "6ad4103d-49aa-55ff-a691-1c7ced9bf9fd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${b}_{n}$ and ${c}_{n}$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-49734/selection/e41e3e9b6e337a04c45a4f24666f1614b2f7b952f3dc76252eb066084abc3bbf" :answer/id #uuid "abf315db-c1da-52cd-a7bd-0fe0bfe090de" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${a}_{n}$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-49734/selection/068ab6c44549303840ef1adf9a99379c384e07623b26bb81c174d3bb8bd9009b" :answer/id #uuid "234e8662-817b-5e07-a98b-ffb415f965a4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${a}_{n}$ and ${b}_{n}$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-49734/selection/83b7363938f4c2d14a7a365d3c0c0baeb6dc576c6e844287a4a4a0fa6426f519" :answer/id #uuid "25faa093-7811-5eec-bd3d-f873e1105f6f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${c}_{n}$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-49734/selection/01a7d213ef6604c7e715a2f07614eb1b2886c6eb9cdc187f710f8faf66f63ac7" :answer/id #uuid "c45892a5-fa4e-59b5-977d-61eb272cef33" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${b}_{n}$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-49734/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "4046cbdf-257a-5bdd-adbd-f63ad89feeef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-49734/selection/e41e3e9b6e337a04c45a4f24666f1614b2f7b952f3dc76252eb066084abc3bbf" :answer-field/choices ["q-49734/selection/8e921bffb50a4e46de83ddac3f98a24bb71d1e68ac0b40cb4fc363fa694725df" "q-49734/selection/e41e3e9b6e337a04c45a4f24666f1614b2f7b952f3dc76252eb066084abc3bbf" "q-49734/selection/068ab6c44549303840ef1adf9a99379c384e07623b26bb81c174d3bb8bd9009b" "q-49734/selection/83b7363938f4c2d14a7a365d3c0c0baeb6dc576c6e844287a4a4a0fa6426f519" "q-49734/selection/01a7d213ef6604c7e715a2f07614eb1b2886c6eb9cdc187f710f8faf66f63ac7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-49734" :question/id #uuid "57946c6b-cbfd-57f6-9f11-08ecdcc2f113" :question/math-academy-id "q-49734" :question/problem "Which of the following sequences, defined for $n>1,$ diverge?\n\n- ${a}_{n}=\\frac{n\\sqrt{n}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n)}$\n- ${b}_{n}=\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡(2n)}{{e}^{n}}$\n- ${c}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{5}}{{e}^{n}}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nTo find the limit of each sequence, we must evaluate them for $n\\to \\infty .$\n\nFor ${a}_{n}=\\frac{n\\sqrt{n}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n)},$ both the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the numerator is growing much faster than the denominator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nFor ${b}_{n}=\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡(2n)}{{e}^{n}},$ both the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{b}_{n}=0.\n$$\n\nFor ${c}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{5}}{{e}^{n}},$ both the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{c}_{n}=0.\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"${a}_{n}$ only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-49734/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "51d50237-f6cf-5c2d-b8d1-335378205a28"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-49734"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-75948/selection/4280bcd0914e40dbdc039bc97ee5d5baafd2654e75e9b692316d9c28c4842856" :answer/id #uuid "524251b7-b5ad-5e06-9944-783aff95dcf9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75948/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "a29159f0-3300-5b4c-8015-adf0d50b0270" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75948/selection/c087f5dc559272d193745a4428553203b94404f681d8529e6e619348c0528d10" :answer/id #uuid "b0c0d996-d20d-529a-a99a-52b143696771" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75948/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "305d791e-48f8-5c83-b0a6-452991cfdb0c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75948/selection/f5ba9cb7e3373c690e498bc176135d7b1a2093e80dfdec0ef9d7cefcc7228be5" :answer/id #uuid "057b99fc-79a5-5517-8523-26c57ecf8038" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The sequence diverges" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75948/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "e84b5bf5-33fc-5944-98f9-2d7eef6825b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-75948/selection/f5ba9cb7e3373c690e498bc176135d7b1a2093e80dfdec0ef9d7cefcc7228be5" :answer-field/choices ["q-75948/selection/4280bcd0914e40dbdc039bc97ee5d5baafd2654e75e9b692316d9c28c4842856" "q-75948/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" "q-75948/selection/c087f5dc559272d193745a4428553203b94404f681d8529e6e619348c0528d10" "q-75948/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-75948/selection/f5ba9cb7e3373c690e498bc176135d7b1a2093e80dfdec0ef9d7cefcc7228be5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-75948" :question/id #uuid "a321a1e4-d59b-5239-a7ef-bd1350c42b74" :question/math-academy-id "q-75948" :question/problem "What is the limit of the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{4{n}^{3}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2n)}+10$ for $n\\ge 1?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nWe calculate the limit of the sequence as $n$ approaches infinity:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{4{n}^{3}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2n)}+10)=10+{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{4{n}^{3}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2n)}\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the numerator is growing much faster than the denominator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{4{n}^{3}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2n)}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{4{n}^{3}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2n)}+10)=\\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sequence diverges." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-75948/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37e00ff7-a91e-5b56-9f3a-24e7318cf004"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-75948"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-123707/selection/f07223adf063c43bfac440bfcfa1f5e709d54202b4c2c9